<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0733">Remark <span data-tag="0733">9.7.8.26</span> <span class="named">(Change of $\mathbb {K}$)</span>.</a> Let $\mathbb {K}_0 \subseteq \mathbb {K}$ be collections of simplicial sets, let $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_0$ be a $\mathbb {K}_0$-cocomplete $\infty $-category, and let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_0 \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ be a functor which exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ as a $\mathbb {K}$-cocompletion of $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_0$ relative to $\mathbb {K}_0$ (see Definition <a data-tag="06ZX" href="/tag/06ZX">9.7.1.16</a>). If $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_0$ is a $\mathbb {K}_0$-cocomplete idempotent, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ is a $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete idempotent. More precisely, if $E \in \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_0$ is an object which exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}_0$ as a $\mathbb {K}_0$-cocomplete idempotent, then $F(E) \in \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ as a $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete idempotent. This follows by combining Remark <a data-tag="072Q" href="/tag/072Q">9.7.8.13</a> with Proposition <a data-tag="0716" href="/tag/0716">9.7.5.3</a>. </p>
